  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' uses the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`, and its time field is `timestamp`. Its main fields include `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (true/false), `FlightDelay`, `FlightDelayMin`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `Origin*` / `Dest*` and `FlightNum`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-c761cf09 Flights' (id `s4-c761cf09-flights-95ba048af771`) with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of the flight count by date.\n   - A bar chart of the flight count for the top 10 carriers.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-c761cf09 Cancelled flights' (id `9b54d1d0-beab-4a2a-b9fc-0b04c517ec24`) as enabled, so it didn't need a separate enable step. It searches `kibana_sample_data_flights` for flights where `Cancelled` is true, runs every minute, and fires when there are more than 10 in a 5-minute window, based on `timestamp`.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the carrier panel to \"Flights by carrier\", and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
